3. INDEPENDENT SOURCE DERIVATIONS

U1 / 1103a / y = 1/|x|

Domain derivation:
  |x| is defined for all real x, but the denominator must not vanish.
  |x|=0 iff x=0. Thus D=(-infinity,0) union (0,infinity).
Sign cases and branches:
  x<0: |x|=-x, so y=-1/x.
  x>0: |x|= x, so y= 1/x.
  Both branches have y>0. They lie in quadrants II and I respectively.
  f(-x)=f(x), so reflection symmetry is about the y-axis.
Range and intercepts:
  Every y>0 occurs, for example at x=1/y; y=0 cannot occur.
  Range=(0,infinity). No x-intercept and no y-intercept.
Monotonicity and shape, derived by hand:
  On x<0, f'=1/x^2>0, f''=-2/x^3>0: increasing and convex upward.
  On x>0, f'=-1/x^2<0, f''=2/x^3>0: decreasing and convex upward.
  These are two reciprocal-curved branches, not straight rays or a V.
Extrema:
  Neither branch has a finite local extremum. No global minimum or maximum.
  Infimum=0 is not attained; the function is unbounded above.
Endpoint/exclusion and limits:
  x->0-: y->+infinity; x->0+: y->+infinity.
  x->-infinity: y->0+; x->+infinity: y->0+.
  Vertical asymptote x=0; horizontal asymptote y=0 on both tails.
  x=0 is excluded by an infinite discontinuity; there is no finite hole at O.
  Neither axis belongs to the function, and neither tail reaches y=0.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-4,1/4), (-2,1/2), (-1,1), (-1/2,2), (-1/4,4),
  (1/4,4), (1/2,2), (1,1), (2,1/2), (4,1/4).
  For instance f(-1/2)=1/(1/2)=2, not -2.
Horizontal shift: none. No finite branch endpoint is included.

U2 / 1103b / y = 1/sqrt(x)

Domain derivation:
  Real sqrt(x) requires x>=0; a nonzero denominator further requires x>0.
  Therefore D=(0,infinity), not [0,infinity) and not R without zero.
Branch and sign:
  Exactly one branch, entirely in quadrant I, with y>0.
  Negative x values have no real function value; there is no reflected branch.
Range and intercepts:
  For each y>0, x=1/y^2>0 satisfies the formula. Range=(0,infinity).
  No x-intercept or y-intercept.
Monotonicity and shape, derived by hand:
  f(x)=x^(-1/2).
  f'=-(1/2)x^(-3/2)<0; f''=(3/4)x^(-5/2)>0.
  Strictly decreasing and convex upward throughout the domain.
  It is not the same curve as 1/x: at x=4 this value is 1/2, not 1/4.
Extrema:
  No finite local/global extremum; infimum=0 not attained; unbounded above.
Limits and exclusions:
  x->0+: y->+infinity; x->+infinity: y->0+.
  Vertical asymptote x=0 approached only from the real-domain right side;
  horizontal asymptote y=0. A real x->0- limit is inapplicable.
  No point at x=0, no finite hole marker, no included endpoint at infinity.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (1/16,4), (1/4,2), (1,1), (4,1/2), (9,1/3), (16,1/4).
  sqrt(1/16)=1/4, hence its reciprocal is 4.
Horizontal shift: none; no y-axis or origin symmetry for the domain.

U3 / 1103c / y = x^3/|x|

Domain derivation:
  The ORIGINAL denominator excludes x=0: D=R without {0}.
Sign-case derivation:
  x<0: x^3/(-x)=-x^2<0.
  x>0: x^3/x=x^2>0.
  On this domain only, f(x)=x|x|. This does not restore x=0.
Branch geometry:
  Left branch is the part of y=-x^2 with x<0 (quadrant III).
  Right branch is the part of y=x^2 with x>0 (quadrant I).
  f(-x)=-f(x): symmetry by a half-turn about O, although O is absent.
Monotonicity and curvature, derived by hand:
  Left: f'=-2x>0 and f''=-2<0, increasing and concave downward.
  Right: f'=2x>0 and f''=2>0, increasing and convex upward.
  Across branches any negative input gives a negative value and any positive
  input gives a positive value, so f is strictly increasing on its whole domain.
Range and intercepts:
  Positive y is obtained at x=sqrt(y); negative y at x=-sqrt(-y).
  Range=R without {0}. No x-intercept or y-intercept.
Extrema:
  No local/global extrema; unbounded both above and below.
  The missing origin is neither a minimum, maximum nor an inflection POINT
  of the actual graph, even though curvature changes across its exclusion.
Limits and exact hole:
  x->0-: y->0-; x->0+: y->0+; the two-sided finite limit is 0.
  Therefore (0,0) is a removable hole and must remain excluded.
  x->-infinity: y->-infinity; x->+infinity: y->+infinity.
  No vertical asymptote at x=0; no horizontal or oblique linear asymptote.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-2,-4), (-1,-1), (-1/2,-1/4),
  (1/2,1/4), (1,1), (2,4).
  At x=-2, (-8)/2=-4. A full upward parabola would have the wrong left branch.
Horizontal shift: none. The branches approach but do not include their shared
finite limiting location O; they must not be connected through a filled origin.

U4 / 1104a / y = sqrt(x^2)+x

Domain and branch derivation:
  x^2>=0 for every real x and sqrt(x^2)=|x|, NOT x for all real x.
  D=R. For x<0, y=-x+x=0; for x>=0, y=x+x=2x.
  Equivalently the horizontal ray x<=0,y=0 and the ray x>=0,y=2x
  meet at ONE included origin. This is a continuous piecewise-linear graph.
Sign, range and intercepts:
  y=0 for every x<=0; y>0 for x>0; never negative.
  Range=[0,infinity). All (x,0) with x<=0 are x-intercepts;
  y-intercept is (0,0).
Monotonicity and shape:
  Constant on (-infinity,0], strictly increasing on [0,infinity),
  and nondecreasing on R.
  Slopes are 0 on x<0 and 2 on x>0; the upward slope jump makes the graph convex.
  A corner, not a smooth turn, occurs at the included origin.
Extrema:
  Global minimum 0 is attained at EVERY x<=0, not just x=0.
  No global maximum; unbounded above. No strict local extrema.
  With the non-strict definition, every interior plateau point x<0 is both
  a local minimum and local maximum; x=0 is a local minimum only.
Limits and endpoints:
  x->-infinity: y=0 exactly; x->+infinity: y->+infinity.
  x->0- and x->0+: y->0=f(0). No hole or excluded finite endpoint.
  These are exact straight rays, not reciprocal curves approaching the axes.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-2,0), (-1,0), (0,0), (1/2,1), (1,2), (2,4).
  At x=-2, sqrt(4)-2=0; at x=2, sqrt(4)+2=4.
Horizontal shift: none. No even or odd origin-centered symmetry.

U5 / 1104b / y = sqrt(x^2)-x

Domain and branch derivation:
  sqrt(x^2)=|x| for all real x, so D=R.
  x<0: y=-x-x=-2x; x>=0: y=x-x=0.
  The ray x<=0,y=-2x and the horizontal ray x>=0,y=0 share ONE
  included origin. Both are exact straight rays; the graph is continuous.
Sign, range and intercepts:
  y>0 for x<0; y=0 for all x>=0; never negative.
  Range=[0,infinity). Every (x,0) with x>=0 is an x-intercept;
  y-intercept is (0,0).
Monotonicity and shape:
  Strictly decreasing on (-infinity,0], constant on [0,infinity),
  and nonincreasing on R.
  Slopes are -2 on x<0 and 0 on x>0, hence an upward slope jump and convexity.
  The included origin is a corner, not an omitted point.
Extrema:
  Global minimum 0 at EVERY x>=0; no global maximum; unbounded above.
  No strict local extrema. Under a non-strict local definition, every
  plateau interior x>0 is both a local minimum and local maximum;
  x=0 is a local minimum only.
Limits and endpoints:
  x->-infinity: y->+infinity; x->+infinity: y=0 exactly.
  Both limits at 0 equal the included value f(0)=0. No holes.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-2,4), (-1,2), (-1/2,1), (0,0), (1,0), (2,0).
  At x=-1, sqrt(1)-(-1)=2, not 0 or -2.
Horizontal shift: none. This is the y-axis reflection of U4, since
  (|-x|+(-x))=|x|-x, but neither U4 nor U5 is itself even/odd.

U6 / 1104c / y = |x+2|

Domain, sign cases and translation:
  D=R. Set x+2=0 to locate the branch break x=-2.
  x<-2: y=-(x+2)=-x-2; x>=-2: y=x+2.
  It is y=|x| translated TWO units LEFT, not up and not right.
  Vertex (-2,0) is included; both slope -1 and slope +1 rays meet there.
Sign, range and intercepts:
  Positive except at x=-2, where it is zero. Range=[0,infinity).
  Sole x-intercept (-2,0); y-intercept (0,2).
Monotonicity and shape:
  Strictly decreasing on (-infinity,-2], strictly increasing on [-2,infinity).
  Convex upward piecewise-linear V; nonsmooth at the vertex.
  f(-2+t)=|t|=f(-2-t), so the symmetry line is x=-2.
  No y-axis even symmetry or origin odd symmetry.
Extrema and limits:
  Unique strict global/local minimum 0 at x=-2; no maximum.
  Both x->-infinity and x->+infinity give y->+infinity.
  Both one-sided limits at -2 equal f(-2)=0.
  No holes, domain gaps, or excluded finite endpoints.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-4,2), (-3,1), (-2,0), (-1,1), (0,2), (1,3).
  These distinguish a horizontal left shift from y=|x|+2.

U7 / 1104d / y = |x-1|

Domain, sign cases and translation:
  D=R. x-1=0 at x=1.
  x<1: y=-(x-1)=1-x; x>=1: y=x-1.
  It is y=|x| translated ONE unit RIGHT, not down or left.
  Included vertex (1,0); left/right rays have slopes -1 and +1.
Sign, range and intercepts:
  Positive except at x=1, where it is zero. Range=[0,infinity).
  Sole x-intercept (1,0); y-intercept (0,1).
Monotonicity and shape:
  Strictly decreasing on (-infinity,1], strictly increasing on [1,infinity).
  Convex upward piecewise-linear V; corner at its included vertex.
  f(1+t)=|t|=f(1-t), so the symmetry line is x=1.
  No y-axis even symmetry or origin odd symmetry.
Extrema and limits:
  Unique strict global/local minimum 0 at x=1; no maximum.
  x->-infinity and x->+infinity both give y->+infinity.
  Both limits at 1 equal f(1)=0; no holes, gaps or excluded finite endpoints.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-1,2), (0,1), (1,0), (2,1), (3,2).
  In particular f(0)=1 and f(1)=0 rule out a vertical downward shift.

U8 / 1105 / y = x + 1/x

Domain and sign derivation:
  1/x requires x!=0: D=(-infinity,0) union (0,infinity).
  f(x)=(x^2+1)/x, and x^2+1>0 for all real x.
  Therefore y has the sign of x: left branch in quadrant III, right in I.
  No real zero, x-intercept, or y-intercept.
  f(-x)=-f(x): half-turn symmetry about O, which is not in the graph.
Range and extrema, independent algebraic derivation:
  For x>0, f(x)-2=(x^2-2x+1)/x=(x-1)^2/x>=0.
  Equality occurs ONLY at x=1; right-branch minimum is (1,2), included.
  For x<0, f(x)+2=(x^2+2x+1)/x=(x+1)^2/x<=0.
  Equality occurs ONLY at x=-1; left-branch maximum is (-1,-2), included.
  On each branch the function is continuous and tends without bound at its
  two outer ends, so all y>=2 on the right and all y<=-2 on the left occur.
  Full range=(-infinity,-2] union [2,infinity).
  No values in (-2,2), but +/-2 ARE attained and must not be omitted.
  (-1,-2) is a strict local maximum and the left branch's global maximum.
  (1,2) is a strict local minimum and the right branch's global minimum.
  Neither is a global extremum of the ENTIRE function: the negative branch
  is unbounded below and the positive branch is unbounded above.
Monotonic intervals, derived by hand:
  f'=1-1/x^2=(x-1)(x+1)/x^2.
  For x<-1, f'>0: increasing.
  For -1<x<0, f'<0: decreasing.
  For 0<x<1, f'<0: decreasing.
  For x>1, f'>0: increasing.
  f'=0 at +/-1, with + to - at -1 and - to + at 1.
  Inclusion of each stationary endpoint in its adjacent monotonic interval
  is valid; zero never belongs to an interval or connects the two branches.
Curvature:
  f''=2/x^3<0 on x<0: left branch concave downward.
  f''=2/x^3>0 on x>0: right branch convex upward.
  This is a two-branch rational curve, not x, 1/x, a V, or one monotone branch.
  No inflection point exists at the excluded origin.
Limits and asymptote-side derivation:
  x->0-: y->-infinity; x->0+: y->+infinity.
  Thus x=0 is a vertical asymptote, not a removable finite hole.
  x->-infinity: y->-infinity; x->+infinity: y->+infinity.
  f(x)-x=1/x -> 0 as x->+/-infinity, so y=x is an oblique asymptote.
  On x<0 the curve lies strictly BELOW y=x; on x>0 strictly ABOVE y=x.
  It never crosses y=x because 1/x cannot equal zero at a finite input.
  There is no horizontal asymptote and no finite endpoint at x=0.
Exact source-derived sample anchors:
  (-4,-17/4), (-2,-5/2), (-1,-2), (-1/2,-5/2), (-1/4,-17/4),
  (1/4,17/4), (1/2,5/2), (1,2), (2,5/2), (4,17/4).
  For example f(1/2)=1/2+2=5/2=f(2), bracketing the minimum at (1,2).
  Similarly f(-1/2)=-5/2=f(-2), bracketing the maximum at (-1,-2).
  The exact reciprocal-input identity f(1/x)=f(x) holds on D, but it must
  not be mistaken for reflection about a vertical line or added domain points.
Horizontal shift: none; adding 1/x to x is not a constant translation.

4. ENDPOINT, SAMPLE AND COVERAGE SAFEGUARDS

All samples above were selected and evaluated by hand directly from the seed.
They are necessary witness anchors for distinguishing sign cases, reciprocal
rates, parabolic versus linear geometry, shifts, and turning branches; a finite
sample set is NOT a proof that an interpolated candidate is globally correct.
The explicit formulas, domain intervals, limits and branch properties supply
that mathematical scope. Future finite pictures may crop infinite tails but
must not imply finite mathematical endpoints or connect across x=0.

Critical domain/point distinctions to preserve:
  U1, U2, U8: x=0 is undefined with infinite one-sided behavior, not a finite O.
  U3: x=0 is undefined despite cancellation and a two-sided limit of zero.
       Its hole is EXACTLY (0,0); its negative branch has negative y.
  U4, U5: origin is included and both exact rays meet; a whole half-line is zero.
  U6: included vertex (-2,0), axis of symmetry x=-2.
  U7: included vertex (1,0), axis of symmetry x=1.
  U8: included stationary points (-1,-2), (1,2), excluded x=0, oblique y=x;
       do not label a branch extremum as the full-domain global extremum.
